<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/10th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fatimid conquest of Egypt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/969">969 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as bloodless conquest of Egypt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Fatimid conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 969 CE was led by the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawhar-al-siqilli">Jawhar al-Siqilli</a>, who took the country with little resistance from the weakened Ikhshidid regime. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> then founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> as its new capital, making Egypt the centre of its power.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> came to power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> in 909 and, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a> claiming descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatima">Fatima</a>, saw themselves as the rightful leaders of the whole Muslim world against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>. Their early invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> failed. By the 960s the Abbasid caliphs had become pawns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a>, while the Ikhshidid regime in Egypt faced Makurian raids from the south, the Lawata Berbers around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, Qarmatian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> attacks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and a severe famine. Fatimid agents worked openly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a>, and many of the local elite came to welcome a Fatimid takeover as an end to the insecurity.</p><p>The death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-misk-kafur">Abu al-Misk Kafur</a> in April 968, without an heir, paralysed the regime. A power-sharing pact under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-tughj-al-ikhshid">al-Ikhshid</a>'s 11-year-old grandson soon broke down, and early in 969 the regent al-Hasan ibn Ubayd Allah returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> effectively without government. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> al-Mu'izz li-Din Allah had prepared for years; the 15th-century historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-maqrizi">al-Maqrizi</a> put the cost at 24 million gold dinars, a figure historians do not take literally.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The army, which Arab sources put at over a hundred thousand men, left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqada">Raqqada</a> on 6 February 969 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawhar-al-siqilli">Jawhar</a>, to whom al-Mu'izz gave full powers. In May it entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a> and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> without resistance. A delegation from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a> sought guarantees, and Jawhar issued a writ of safe-conduct, the amān, promising to respect the rights of the notables and people and to take up the jihād against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>. The Ikhshidid soldiers tried to bar his crossing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> between 29 June and 3 July and were overcome. Pro-Fatimid agents took control of Fustat, Jawhar entered on 6 July, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khutba">Friday prayer</a> was read in the name of al-Mu'izz on 9 July.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawhar-al-siqilli">Jawhar</a> governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> as viceroy for four years, disarming the Ikhshidid troops and confiscating their property, quelling rebellions and beginning a new capital. An advance into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> ended in disaster: Ja'far ibn Fallah was defeated and killed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarmatians">Qarmatians</a> in August 971, and their invasion was fought off just north of the new city. Al-Mu'izz set out from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> with his court, his treasure and the coffins of his ancestors, and on 10 June 973 he crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> and went straight to his capital, named al-Qāhira al-Muʿizzīya, which became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. It remained the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> abolished the regime in 1171.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fatimid conquest of Egypt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
